  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nThis afternoon, an upper level low is located near the Twin \nCities, with lingering rain and stratus to its northwest over \nmuch of west central MN. Rain will slowly come to an end as the \nlow tracks towards the Great Lakes tonight. In its wake, a mix \nof stratus and fog is expected to develop as high pressure \nnudges in from the west. Thereafter, expansive ridging over the \nwestern CONUS will propagate eastward, bringing ever warmer \ntemperatures and generally dry conditions over the next seven \ndays. Temperatures over the course of the week will warm from \nthe upper 30s/40s on Thursday, into the 40s on Friday and then \nwell into the 50s from Saturday through early next week. The \nridge does flatten a bit over the weekend, but any weak waves \npropagating through the flow still look to remain well to our \nnorth. Early next week, another ridge over the western US pushes\neastward out of the Rockies. 850 mb temperatures Sunday night \ninto Monday are near their climatological max over the western \nDakotas, with 90th percentile values extending eastward into our\nsouthern FA. With 850mb temperatures of 11 to 13 degrees \nCelsius Monday afternoon, portions of southeastern ND and west \ncentral MN are forecasted to have highs push to near 60 degrees.\nCloud cover, along with a quick passing cold front tracking \nsoutheastward Monday afternoon both may limit just how warm we \nget. If the front slows down and clouds can hold off, \ntemperatures may climb a few degrees over 60. This is supported \nby the 75th percentile of the NBM, which shows highs in our \nregion generally in the 61 to 63 degree range. For reference, \naverage high temperatures by early next week are on either side \nof 40 degrees (upper 30s near the Canadian Border, low 40s near \nthe SD border). Behind the front, temperatures will drop just a \nbit into the 50s, which is still well above average. \n\n...Rain Slowly Ending Late Wednesday...\n\nAfter three days of stratus and on and off rain, the upper low \nresponsible for the latest dreary weather will finally push \nnortheast tonight. This will bring an end to rainfall. Cloud \ncover may not be as fast to leave, with stratus or fog forming \novernight. Low level winds to look to remain a bit elevated, \nwhich may help limit the areal extent of fog and keep us with \nmore stratus instead. Nonetheless, with recent rainfall, a few \npatches of fog are forecast overnight into Thursday morning \nacross much of eastern ND into adjacent portions of northwestern\nMN.",
